{"sourceSha256": "db6dbf6cef6f0590afc5e768cd8ba96853b41f08be3e9003f8eac208c151662d", "text": "Dr Jack Thomas\nGastroenterologist\nDepartment Of Gastroenterologist                                                                              Comment [teacher1]: Gastroenterolog\n                                                                                                              y\nCity Hospital\nMain Road\nStillwater\n\n21/02/2015\n\nDear Dr Thomas,\nRe: Mr Patrick Newton,                                  D.O.B: 06/07/1989\n\nThank you for seeing Mr Newton, a 25-year-old accountant, whose features are suggestive of inflammatory\nbowel disease. Your further management would be highly appreciated.\n\nMr Newton is single and lives with his parents. Unfortunately, he is a heavy smoker despite playing squash\nregularly. Please note, he has a family history of Crohn's disease. He has had intermittent bilateral wrist\njoint pain for the last six months.\n\nOn 21/02/2015, he presented to me complaining of a four-month history of chronic mild diarrhea together\nwith mild pain in the right lower quadrant of his abdomen. Moreover, he had a decreased appetite, the\nreason why he lost a weight of three kilograms over the past four months. Although he tried diet\nmodification and taking ibuprofen, his symptoms persist. His examination was unremarkable except for a        Comment [teacher2]: took\ngeneralised abdominal tenderness. His laboratory tests revealed an increase in the white cells count along\nwith a decrease in the red cells count and the haemoglobin. As well, his CRP and ESR were mildly elevated\nin addition to a positive faecal occult blood test. The patient was advised to stop smoking.\n\nIn light of the above, the patent is being referred today for further assessment in order to rule out\ninflammatory bowel disease. For any queries, please contact me.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nDoctor\n\n                                     Report\n Word length                  19